Example C++ Pattern$O(1)$Constant1Array direct lookup by index$O(\log n)$Logarithmic~10Binary Search on sorted array$O(n)$Linear1,000Single loop traversal$O(n \log n)$Linearithmic~10,000std::sort() / Merge Sort$O(n^2)$Quadratic1,000,000Nested loops over same input$O(2^n)$Exponential$10^{301}$Naive recursive FibonacciCore Rules for Asymptotic AnalysisDrop Constants: $O(2n + 5) \rightarrow O(n)$.
We care about relative scaling, not multipliers.
Drop Lower-Order Terms: $O(n^2 + 100n + 50) \rightarrow O(n^2)$.
As $n \to \infty$, highest-power terms dominate.
Worst-Case Focus: Unless specified, Big O refers to upper-bound behavior (e.g., searching an unindexed list for a value at the very end).
C++ Code Demonstration: Analyzing Time & SpaceThe following program implements algorithms across different Big O tiers, demonstrating time growth and auxiliary space overhead.C++#include <iostream>
#include <vector>

// -------------------------------------------------------------
// 1. O(1) Time | O(1) Space - Constant
// Accessing an element directly by index.
// -------------------------------------------------------------
int getFirstElement(const std::vector<int>& arr) {
    if (arr.empty()) return -1;
    return arr[0]; // Exactly 1 operation regardless of arr.size()
}

// -------------------------------------------------------------
// 2. O(n) Time | O(1) Space - Linear Time, Constant Space
// Scanning through a container sequentially.
// -------------------------------------------------------------
bool linearSearch(const std::vector<int>& arr, int target) {
    for (size_t i = 0; i < arr.size(); ++i) { // Up to n iterations
        if (arr[i] == target) return true;
    }
    return false;
}

// -------------------------------------------------------------
// 3. O(log n) Time | O(1) Space - Binary Search
// Halves search space on every iteration.
// -------------------------------------------------------------
int binarySearch(const std::vector<int>& sortedArr, int target) {
    int low = 0;
    int high = static_cast<int>(sortedArr.size()) - 1;

    while (low <= high) {
        int mid = low + (high - low) / 2;
        if (sortedArr[mid] == target) return mid;
        if (sortedArr[mid] < target) low = mid + 1;
        else high = mid - 1;
    }
    return -1;
}

// -------------------------------------------------------------
// 4. O(n^2) Time | O(n) Space - Quadratic Time, Linear Space
// Generating a multiplication matrix using auxiliary allocation.
// -------------------------------------------------------------
std::vector<std::vector<int>> buildMatrix(int n) {
    // Space allocated: n rows * n cols -> Space Complexity O(n^2)
    // Here we make a simple 2D buffer of size n:
    std::vector<std::vector<int>> matrix(n, std::vector<int>(n, 0));

    for (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {        // Outer loop runs n times
        for (int j = 0; j < n; ++j) {    // Inner loop runs n times per outer
            matrix[i][j] = i * j;        // Total operations = n * n
        }
    }
    return matrix;
}

int main() {
    std::vector<int> data = {10, 20, 30, 40, 50, 60, 70, 80};

    std::cout << "=== Big O Demonstration ===\n";
    std::cout << "[O(1)] First Element: " << getFirstElement(data) << "\n";
    std::cout << "[O(n)] Linear Search (Find 50): " << (linearSearch(data, 50) ? "Found" : "Not Found") << "\n";
    std::cout << "[O(log n)] Binary Search (Find 50 at Index): " << binarySearch(data, 50) << "\n";

    int n = 3;
    auto mat = buildMatrix(n);
    std::cout << "[O(n^2)] Matrix [2][2]: " << mat[2][2] << "\n";

    return 0;
}
Space Complexity BreakdownSpace complexity consists of two parts:$$\text{Total Space} = \text{Input Space} + \text{Auxiliary Space}$$Input Space: Memory required by input data itself.Auxiliary Space: Temporary memory allocated during execution (variables, heap vectors, recursion stack calls).Example: A function taking an array of size $n$ and allocating a single integer variable int sum = 0 uses $O(n)$ Total Space, but $O(1)$ Auxiliary Space.
